WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.707
Picture a shop that keeps its sales in notebooks, in pen, one page per day.

2
00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:12.313
One evening, 30 refunds come in, 10 from each of the last three days.

3
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:13.983
You can't rub out ink.

4
00:00:13.983 --> 00:00:15.169
So what do you do?

5
00:00:15.169 --> 00:00:18.397
Copy the three pages out again, with the changes?

6
00:00:18.397 --> 00:00:22.284
Or leave them alone, and pin a slip of corrections to each?

7
00:00:22.284 --> 00:00:23.404
Hold that choice.

8
00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:26.222
A payments company meets exactly this.

9
00:00:26.222 --> 00:00:33.375
Every evening, a file of refunds arrives: 30 payments whose status must change from captured to refunded.

10
00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:43.585
In an ordinary database, the program that stores tables and answers questions about them, one UPDATE command changes the 30 rows in place.

11
00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:43.948
Done.

12
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.541
But our table is an Iceberg table.

13
00:00:46.541 --> 00:00:53.219
Apache Iceberg is a free format for tables made of plain files, and those files are never edited.

14
00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:58.727
The rows sit in Parquet files, a common file format for tables stored by column.

15
00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:06.962
The columns are squeezed, and a summary at the end of the file records where each one sits, and its smallest and largest values.

16
00:01:06.962 --> 00:01:09.659
Change one value, and all of that is wrong.

17
00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:11.604
So the file is the page in ink.

18
00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:17.841
Every change is written as new files, and a commit, a new snapshot of the table, makes them visible.

19
00:01:17.841 --> 00:01:19.429
The command is MERGE INTO.

20
00:01:19.429 --> 00:01:22.972
It matches the refunds to the table on the payment number.

21
00:01:22.972 --> 00:01:25.904
Under it, there are two ways to write the files.

22
00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:27.828
Way one: copy the page out.

23
00:01:27.828 --> 00:01:34.169
Find every file that holds a row to change, read it whole, and write a new copy with the change made.

24
00:01:34.169 --> 00:01:37.183
The commit swaps the old files for the new ones.

25
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.575
To change 30 rows, how many unchanged rows did it copy?

26
00:01:41.575 --> 00:01:42.789
Pause and guess.

27
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:47.170
299,970.

28
00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:54.132
Three whole files, 3.1 MB, rewritten for 30 rows.

29
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:57.819
This is called copy-on-write, and it's Iceberg's default.

30
00:01:57.819 --> 00:02:02.283
Readers notice nothing, because the table is still just plain data files.

31
00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:05.793
But it breaks when changes are small and frequent.

32
00:02:05.793 --> 00:02:13.908
Corrections every 5 minutes, against files of 512 MB, and you're rewriting gigabytes an hour to change a few hundred rows.

33
00:02:13.908 --> 00:02:17.633
That's copying out the whole notebook to fix one smudge.

34
00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:19.191
Way two: pin a slip.

35
00:02:19.191 --> 00:02:22.519
Back to the original files, and leave them alone.

36
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:31.487
Write the new versions of the 30 rows into small new data files, plus delete files that say which old rows no longer count.

37
00:02:31.633 --> 00:02:39.001
Each line of a delete file names a data file and a row position in it, such as row 1,000 of the day 1 file.

38
00:02:39.001 --> 00:02:41.204
That's called a position delete.

39
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:48.846
The same 30 refunds wrote 6.4 KB of new rows and 7.4 KB of delete files.

40
00:02:48.846 --> 00:02:52.052
About 14 KB, instead of 3.1 MB.

41
00:02:52.052 --> 00:02:54.327
This is merge-on-read.

42
00:02:54.567 --> 00:02:56.017
And here's the catch.

43
00:02:56.017 --> 00:02:58.021
The cost moved to the reader.

44
00:02:58.021 --> 00:03:04.031
Every read of a data file must also read its delete files, and skip the rows they list.

45
00:03:04.031 --> 00:03:06.241
One batch turned 3 files into 9.

46
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.465
Here the notebook picture breaks.

47
00:03:08.465 --> 00:03:10.906
You'd read a slip once and remember it.

48
00:03:10.906 --> 00:03:14.975
The table's readers apply every delete file again, on every read.

49
00:03:15.167 --> 00:03:17.259
And corrections keep coming.

50
00:03:17.259 --> 00:03:24.881
After five batches, each day has 5 small data files and 5 delete files, and every read opens 33 files.

51
00:03:24.881 --> 00:03:27.496
That's a lot of slips for one page.

52
00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:31.175
Iceberg's format version 3 trims part of that bill.

53
00:03:31.175 --> 00:03:38.191
It keeps one bitmap per data file, a row of bits with a 1 for each deleted row, called a deletion vector.

54
00:03:38.333 --> 00:03:45.658
A later delete in the same file joins the same bitmap, so a reader loads at most one per data file.

55
00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:49.653
In the demo, all three vectors fit in one 1.7 KB file.

56
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.162
So, copy the page or pin a slip?

57
00:03:52.162 --> 00:03:58.169
You choose per table, and per kind of change, with settings such as write.merge.mode.

58
00:03:58.367 --> 00:04:00.771
Rare, large changes: copy-on-write.

59
00:04:00.771 --> 00:04:03.244
Frequent, small ones: merge-on-read.

60
00:04:03.244 --> 00:04:11.144
And remember that merge-on-read sends its readers a bill, which a cleanup job, called compaction, has to pay later.

61
00:04:11.367 --> 00:04:13.025
So the moral of the story.

62
00:04:13.025 --> 00:04:16.023
A file that can't be edited leaves two choices.

63
00:04:16.023 --> 00:04:18.829
Copy the page, and pay once, when you write.

64
00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:22.145
Or pin a slip, and pay a little every time you read.

65
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:23.504
One quick check.

66
00:04:23.504 --> 00:04:29.784
A table gets a batch of corrections every five minutes, and a report reads it once a night.

67
00:04:29.784 --> 00:04:30.474
Which way?

68
00:04:30.474 --> 00:04:31.579
Pause and think.

69
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:35.703
Merge-on-read.

70
00:04:35.703 --> 00:04:40.540
The changes are small and frequent, and the reader comes only once a night.

71
00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:42.991
Just tidy the slips away now and then.
